# signing key — a leak lets anyone sign a mod as you
*.biprivatekey
keys/*.biprivatekey

# build output
build/out/
native/build/
# WHAT LIVES IN A @Mod FOLDER, AND WHY GIT SEES PART OF IT.
#
# A @Mod folder is mostly build output, and none of that is committed: the
# pbo, its signature and the key copy beside it are all ignored below.
#
# Two files in there are NOT output, and both must survive a rebuild:
#
#   mod.cpp    source. The name, author, version and the description a server
#              owner reads in the launcher. Written by hand.
#   meta.cpp   the Workshop item id. Neither source nor output -- bookkeeping.
#              Lose it and the next publish forks a second Workshop item
#              instead of updating the one that exists.
#
# They are tracked in place because Publisher wants them in that folder and
# nothing in this toolchain assembles it: mod_build writes addons/ and leaves
# the rest alone. Keeping them elsewhere would need a copy step that does not
# exist, and a copy step is a way to publish a stale mod.cpp.
#
# So the folder itself is not ignored -- ignoring a directory stops git
# descending into it, and a "!@OpenZone_Radio/mod.cpp" exception below would
# not have rescued anything.
*.pbo

# The key COPIES inside each @Mod are build output: the pack step puts them
# there next to the pbo it just signed. The source of truth is keys/ in the
# repository root, and committing the copies meant the same file lived in git
# four times, three of them regenerated by every build.
@*/keys/
*.bisign
*.log
*.RPT

# local
.compile-check-profile/
.dayz-mcp/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# машинний профіль MCP: шляхи цієї машини, не комітиться
dayz-mcp.local.toml
